# Build output. Each app builds into its own build/; nothing under one is tracked.
build/
*.o
*.a
*.elf
*.prx

# Release staging: each app's `dist` target writes the artifacts CI publishes here.
dist/

# A title's upstream source, fetched at the revision its `upstream.lock` names. The lock,
# patches and shim beside it are tracked; the fetched tree is not. `upstream-<name>/` is a
# second origin, fetched from `upstream-<name>.lock` (`common/upstream.mk`).
upstream/
upstream-*/

# Python is not used here, so there is no bytecode rule; a stray file shows in `git status`.

# Claude Code's per-person overrides.
.claude/settings.local.json

# Editor and OS files.
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
*.swo
*~

# Artifacts the build cannot reproduce, staged by hand from a host toolchain a payload
# build does not have. They sit outside `build/` because make clears that.
prebuilt/

# Except Ship of Harkinian's, which is tracked.
#
# It is the port's own assets - its fonts, its UI textures, libultraship's shaders - and
# libultraship refuses to start without it. It carries nothing from a cartridge: it is built
# by `extract_assets.py --norom`, and the names say so, being things like
# `gTitleRandomizerSubtitleTex` and `gRocsFeatherTex` that exist in the port and not in the
# game.
#
# Tracked because the alternative is that the title has no release at all. Producing it needs
# a host build of ZAPD with its desktop dependencies, which CI does not have and should not
# grow, so an untracked copy means every published archive for this title is one that cannot
# start. 4.5MB against that is cheap.
#
# It is a derived artifact pinned to an upstream revision, so it can go stale silently. It
# does not: the archive carries a `portVersion` entry and the Makefile refuses to package one
# that disagrees with `UPSTREAM_REF`.
!src/oops-titles/ship-of-harkinian/prebuilt/
!src/oops-titles/ship-of-harkinian/prebuilt/soh.o2r
!src/oops-titles/2-ship-2-harkinian/prebuilt/
!src/oops-titles/2-ship-2-harkinian/prebuilt/2ship.o2r

# Host-side build output. Same reason as `build/`, a newer directory name that the
# rule above did not cover - 451 files of object code were sitting untracked behind it.
build-hosted/
